+    {
+      "key": "dyadic-tree-critical-survival",
+      "namespace": "probability.percolation",
+      "logical_name": "dyadic-branching-tree-critical-survival-outside-quasitransitivity",
+      "kind": "theorem",
+      "title": "A bounded-degree non-quasi-transitive tree survives at its bond threshold",
+      "statement": "Let T be the rooted tree in which each vertex at depth n-1 has 8 children when n is a positive power of two and 2 children otherwise. T has maximum degree 9, is not quasi-transitive, has Bernoulli bond threshold p_c(T)=1/2, and the root belongs to an infinite open cluster at p=1/2 with probability at least 4/5. Thus bounded degree and p_c<1 alone do not imply critical extinction.",
+      "proof_text": "Put m(n)=1+floor(log_2 n). The n-th generation has 2^n 4^{m(n)} vertices. For p<1/2, the expected open generation size is at most 4n^2(2p)^n, which tends to zero; the same bound from each fixed vertex rules out all infinite clusters. At p=1/2 let Z_n count root-connected generation-n vertices and M_n=E Z_n=4^{m(n)}. Fresh disjoint offspring edges give E[Z_n^2 | F_{n-1}]=(b_n p)^2 Z_{n-1}^2+b_n p(1-p)Z_{n-1}, hence E Z_n^2/M_n^2=1+(1/2)sum_{j=1}^n 1/M_j. The infinite sum is sum_{k>=0}2^k/4^{k+1}=1/2. Cauchy–Schwarz yields P(Z_n>0)>=4/5 for every n; continuity and local finiteness give root survival at least 4/5. The root is the unique vertex of degree 8, so all automorphisms fix it and preserve infinitely many distance levels; T is not quasi-transitive. This is w436's construction independently checked by w437, and does not contradict P0043.",
